NNAAMMEE
     mmaann..ccoonnff — configuration file for man

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     This is the configuration file for the man(1), apropos(1), and
     makewhatis(8) utilities.  Its presence, and all directives, are optional.

     This file is an ASCII text file.  Leading whitespace on lines, lines
     starting with ‘#’, and blank lines are ignored.  Words are separated by
     whitespace.  The first word on each line is the name of a configuration
     directive.

     The following directives are supported:

     mmaannppaatthh _p_a_t_h
             Override the default search _p_a_t_h for man(1), apropos(1), and
             makewhatis(8).  It can be used multiple times to specify multiple
             paths, with the order determining the manual page search order.

             Each path is a tree containing subdirectories whose names consist
             of the strings ‘man’ and/or ‘cat’ followed by the names of sec‐
             tions, usually single digits.  The former are supposed to contain
             unformatted manual pages in mdoc(7) and/or man(7) format; file
             names should end with the name of the section preceded by a dot.
             The latter should contain preformatted manual pages; file names
             should end with ‘.0’.

             Creating a mandoc.db(5) database with makewhatis(8) in each di‐
             rectory configured with mmaannppaatthh is recommended and necessary for
             apropos(1) to work, and also for man(1) on operating systems like
             OpenBSD that install each manual page with only one file name in
             the file system, even if it documents multiple utilities or func‐
             tions.

     oouuttppuutt _o_p_t_i_o_n [_v_a_l_u_e]
             Configure the default value of an output option.  These direc‐
             tives are overridden by the --OO command line options of the same
             names.  For details, see the mandoc(1) manual.

             _o_p_t_i_o_n      _v_a_l_u_e      used by --TT     purpose

             ffrraaggmmeenntt    none       hhttmmll           print only body
             iinncclluuddeess    string     hhttmmll           path to header files
             iinnddeenntt      integer    aasscciiii, uuttff88    left margin
             mmaann         string     hhttmmll           path for Xr links
             ppaappeerr       string     ppss, ppddff        paper size
             ssttyyllee       string     hhttmmll           CSS file
             ttoocc         none       hhttmmll           print table of contents
             wwiiddtthh       integer    aasscciiii, uuttff88    right margin

FFIILLEESS
     /etc/man.conf
     /etc/examples/man.conf

EEXXAAMMPPLLEESS
     The following configuration file reproduces the defaults: installing it
     is equivalent to not having a mmaann..ccoonnff file at all.

           manpath /usr/share/man
           manpath /usr/X11R6/man
           manpath /usr/local/man

SSEEEE AALLSSOO
     apropos(1), man(1), makewhatis(8)

HHIISSTTOORRYY
     A relatively complicated mmaann..ccoonnff file format first appeared in
     4.3BSD-Reno.  For OpenBSD 5.8, it was redesigned from scratch, aiming for
     simplicity.

